  "text": "The study looks at two historic ways of writing the language spoken on a high‑altitude plateau. One writing system was created after a South‑Asian alphabet was introduced in the 7th century; it borrowed heavily from that source language and was later standardized by a ruler in the early 9th century, becoming the formal literary form used in religious texts. The second system, unrelated to the South‑Asian influence, directly reflected how people spoke in the 9th century and is known from manuscript finds in Central Asian desert sites and a Chinese desert city. Over time this older script mixed with the formal one, giving rise to a non‑religious written variety. While many written records survive, spoken examples after the 10th century are rare, except for a bilingual glossary that records the spoken form of a regional dialect using Chinese characters. This glossary, despite phonetic mismatches, provides a clear picture of the 16th‑century spoken dialect of a province in the eastern part of the plateau, and the forms it records may still be heard today."
